<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  OBD Platform - Phase C: SIP profile for a local SIP test endpoint.

  TEMPLATE - rendered at container start by infra/freeswitch-endpoint/scripts/entrypoint.sh.

  This is the whole "softphone". Everything about it is deliberately explicit,
  because the point of Phase C is to know exactly what the platform switch is
  really talking to:

    * ONE profile, named obd-endpoint, bound to 0.0.0.0 on 5060. It replaces
      the image's stock internal/external profiles, so the endpoint has no
      stock trunk, no stock IPv6 profile and no stock websocket binding to
      confuse a packet trace. (Replacing the stock list is also how the stock
      IPv6 profiles that bind [::1]:5060 were removed on the platform switch -
      see 13-TROUBLESHOOTING.md.)

    * auth-calls is false: the platform switch calls this endpoint WITHOUT
      digest credentials, because an endpoint is not a subscriber. Registration
      the other way - this endpoint registering to the platform - is what
      carries credentials, and that is the gateway below.

    * context is obd-endpoint, defined in dialplan/obd-endpoint.xml. It answers,
      emits a known DTMF sequence, waits, and hangs up. Nothing else.

    * sip-ip and rtp-ip are left UNSET on purpose. Setting sip-ip pins the
      address advertised in Contact, and setting rtp-ip pins the address
      advertised in SDP; pinning either to 0.0.0.0 would tell the far end to
      send media to 0.0.0.0, which is unroutable. Left unset, the endpoint
      advertises the container address, which the platform switch can reach
      because they share the obd-telephony network.

  EVERY <param> MUST SIT INSIDE <settings>
  Observed in Phase C: with the parameters written directly under <profile>,
  mod_sofia loaded the profile, found it in the compiled configuration, and
  then refused to start it:

      [ERR] sofia.c:4567 No Settings, check the new config!

  The failure is quiet in the worst way - FreeSWITCH starts, reports UP, has
  healthy ESL, and the endpoint simply never listens on any SIP port. A port
  check or a packet trace is the only thing that reveals it.

  THE DTMF PARAMETERS ARE NOT DECORATION
  rfc2833-pt pins the RTP payload type used for RFC 4733 in-band DTMF events.
  It MUST match the platform switch's value (101 there, and 101 here) or the
  two ends negotiate DTMF on different payload types and the tones are never
  decoded - while SIP signalling looks perfectly healthy. liberal-dtmf and
  dtmf-duration mirror the platform so an event that is generated on one side
  is decoded identically on the other.

  THE GATEWAY IS HOW THIS ENDPOINT REGISTERS
  A FreeSWITCH instance becomes a SIP client by configuring a gateway with
  register=true. That produces a real REGISTER, a real 401 digest challenge
  and a real 200 OK against the platform switch - i.e. this endpoint proves the
  same registration flow a softphone would, from the other direction.

  realm and proxy are set to the platform switch's ADDRESS, not its network
  alias. This matters and was learned the hard way: the platform switch pins its
  authentication realm to its own domain, which is its container address, and
  FreeSWITCH files registrations as user@realm. Registering via the alias
  "freeswitch" would put the alias in the To header, and the realm - and
  therefore the registration key - would become "freeswitch", which is not a
  domain the platform switch knows. The entrypoint resolves the address at
  start-up so this stays correct across container recreates.

  Note <gateways> is a child of <profile>, NOT of the profile's <settings> and
  not of sofia.conf.xml. mod_sofia resolves the path
  sofia.conf -> profiles -> profile[@name] -> gateways -> gateway, so a
  gateways block anywhere else compiles without error and is silently ignored.

  caller-id-in-from is true so the platform switch sees the endpoint as a real
  peer with an identity rather than an anonymous one.
-->
<profile name="obd-endpoint">

  <settings>
    <param name="debug" value="0"/>
    <param name="sip-trace" value="no"/>
    <param name="sip-capture" value="no"/>

    <param name="sip-port" value="5060"/>
    <param name="listen-ip" value="0.0.0.0"/>

    <param name="dialplan" value="XML"/>
    <param name="context" value="obd-endpoint"/>

    <!-- An endpoint is called without credentials; it authenticates itself
         when it registers, via the gateway below. -->
    <param name="auth-calls" value="false"/>
    <param name="auth-subscriptions" value="false"/>
    <param name="challenge-realm" value="$${domain}"/>

    <!-- Codecs: PCMU first, so the platform switch's outbound-codec-prefs
         match on the first choice and the SDP stays predictable. -->
    <param name="inbound-codec-prefs" value="PCMU,PCMA"/>
    <param name="outbound-codec-prefs" value="PCMU,PCMA"/>

    <!-- Must equal the platform switch's rfc2833-pt (101), or in-band DTMF
         is negotiated on two different payload types and never decoded. -->
    <param name="rfc2833-pt" value="101"/>
    <param name="liberal-dtmf" value="true"/>
    <param name="dtmf-duration" value="2000"/>

    <param name="inbound-codec-negotiation" value="generous"/>
    <param name="rtp-timer-name" value="soft"/>

    <!-- Defence in depth, same ACL as the platform switch. -->
    <param name="apply-inbound-acl" value="obd-dev-acl"/>
  </settings>

  <gateways>
    <gateway name="platform-switch">
      <param name="proxy" value="@@PLATFORM_PROXY@@"/>
      <param name="realm" value="@@PLATFORM_REALM@@"/>

      <param name="register" value="true"/>
      <param name="register-transport" value="udp"/>
      <param name="register-retry-interval" value="30"/>
      <param name="register-max-attempts" value="0"/>
      <param name="expires" value="300"/>

      <param name="username" value="@@EXTENSION@@"/>
      <param name="password" value="@@SIP_PASSWORD@@"/>

      <param name="caller-id-in-from" value="true"/>
      <param name="outbound-codec-prefs" value="PCMU"/>
    </gateway>
  </gateways>

</profile>
